840,"bytes":40434},"NLD":{"file":"NLD.json.gz","n":6840,"bytes":43465}},"coverage":{"sample_covered":["DEU","DNK","SWE","NLD","FRA","GBR","USA"],"sample_missing":[],"sample_span":{"DEU":["2001","2025"],"DNK":["2001","2024"],"SWE":["2001","2025"],"NLD":["2001","2024"],"FRA":["2001","2025"],"GBR":["2001","2025"],"USA":["2001","2025"]},"oecd_missing":["COL","CRI","MEX"],"oecd_covered":35,"oecd_total":38},"merged_from":[{"flow":"DSD_TAXBEN_PTR@DF_PTRSA","agency":"OECD.ELS.JAI","new_observations":99592,"overlap_agreed":0,"overlap_differed":0},{"flow":"DSD_TAXBEN_PTR@DF_PTRCCUB","agency":"OECD.ELS.JAI","new_observations":1436400,"overlap_agreed":0,"overlap_differed":0},{"flow":"DSD_TAXBEN_PTR@DF_PTRCCSA","agency":"OECD.ELS.JAI","new_observations":79800,"overlap_agreed":0,"overlap_differed":0}],"desc_html":"What happens to your income when you take a job. The number is the share of the earnings from a new job that never reaches you, because taxes start and benefits stop at the same time. At 100 per cent, working leaves you no better off than not working.<br>This is the measurable core of the argument about whether benefits discourage work, and the answer is not the one either side usually assumes: rates are high almost everywhere, and they are highest for the people entering low-paid work, not for high earners.<br>The <b>Contribution</b> dial breaks the rate into its causes -- taxes starting, benefits withdrawn, childcare fees beginning. For a lone parent taking a minimum-wage job, childcare fees alone often exceed everything the tax system takes.<br>Switch <b>Benefit type</b> between unemployment insurance and guaranteed minimum income, and set how long the person has been out of work. Under Advanced are the assumptions behind the simulation: whether housing benefit and social assistance top-ups are claimed, whether childcare is used, and the ages of the children.<br>These are modelled households, not survey observations. OECD computes what its tax-benefit rules imply for a specified family in a specified situation.","desc_text":"The share of earnings from a new job lost to taxes starting and benefits stopping, for modelled households leaving unemployment or minimum income.","source_notes":["Four OECD tables are merged here: participation tax rates when claiming unemployment benefits or guaranteed minimum income, each with and without childcare costs. They share a structure and differ only in which scenario codes they carry, so they are one dataset rather than four near-duplicates. No overlapping value disagreed.","These are simulations from OECD's tax-benefit models, not observations of real households. They assume full take-up of every benefit the household is entitled to, which real take-up never reaches, so they overstate what an average claimant actually loses by starting work."],"hidden_dims":{"AGE_CHILDREN":"Y4_6","INCOME_PART":"_Z","CHILDCARE_USE":"NO","SOC_ASS_BENEFIT":"YES","HOUSE_BENEFIT":"NO","TEMP_INTOWORK_BENEFIT":"NO"},"default_bundle":{"areas":["DEU","DNK","SWE","NLD","FRA","GBR","USA"],"bytes":971,"n":76,"picks":{"INCOME_PART":"_Z","UNEMP_DURATION":"_Z","SOC_ASS_BENEFIT":"_Z","CONTRIBUTION":"_T"},"partial":true}}